---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import Landscape from '@assets/landscape.jpeg'
---

<section class="mx-auto mt-8 w-[90%] sm:mt-12 md:text-lg 2xl:w-[60%]">
  <h2 class="text-xl md:text-3xl">🗃️ About me?</h2>
  <p class="mt-3">
    I started programming in 2018 with PHP and C++ through school programs.
    Later, I switched to JavaScript to pursue web development. I also worked
    with Java and C# because of college (not exactly by choice).
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Over the last few years, I have been enjoying working with <strong
      >Go</strong
    >, <strong>Rust</strong>, and <strong>C</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-[360px_1fr]">
    <div>
      <Image
        src={Landscape}
        alt="Landscape"
        class="mx-auto rounded-sm border"
      />
      <p
        class="mt-3 w-full text-center text-xs opacity-80 sm:text-sm md:text-left"
      >
        Mandatory scenery picture.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg md:text-2xl">
        Programming Languages & Web Frameworks
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-3">
        I have worked with a fair number of programming languages (I did not
        list all of them). I do not have a favorite; most of the time, I choose
        whichever language I consider the best fit for the task at hand.
        However, I enjoy using each of them in their own way.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        I enjoy understanding their ergonomics and the reasoning behind their
        design decisions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6">
    That said, putting aside the romanticism, when it comes to building useful
    things, I prefer to be practical.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg md:text-2xl">Express? Fastify? Django? Fiber</h3>
  <p class="mt-3">
    I know all of them are from different languages and have "different" goals.
    But Go's design makes your life easier. At the beginning, declaring
    public/private visibility using uppercase or lowercase letters seems weird,
    but after a few days, you understand that it is actually an incredibly
    simple mechanism.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    No extra public/private keywords. Naming conventions become a little bit
    odd, but you can survive it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The package system is extremely explicit. Stick with some basic design
    principles, and you'll be able to write useful deep modules with ease.
    Almost any design decision becomes explicit and is always present in your
    code, which is something important when it comes to maintainability.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg md:text-2xl">Is Go the best programming language?</h3>
  <p class="mt-3">
    Best language? That's nothing more than a myth. Though, since I mainly take
    care of backend stuff, Go is performant enough, easy to read, easy to write,
    and easy to maintain.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-[1fr_240px]">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl md:text-3xl">Technical Focus</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        My work has primarily involved backend development with REST APIs,
        covering areas such as SQL query design, third-party library
        integration, service-to-service communication, authentication flows, and
        other backend development tasks.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        I am also familiar with Docker, Linux, scripting, package managers, Git,
        and cloud providers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <img
        src="code-contribution.svg"
        alt="undraw code contribution"
        class="mx-auto"
        width={200}
      />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
